# toolkit.platform.browser — nebula1.0 Phase 4 (`<link>`): opening a URL in
# whatever the host has as a "browser". Just the interface and the safe
# default here -- each TARGET's own concrete UrlOpener lives in its own
# file (toolkit.render.web.canvas2d_renderer.Canvas2DUrlOpener, toolkit.
# platform.native_browser.NativeUrlOpener), one implementer per file,
# matching this toolkit's existing Canvas/Renderer convention (see
# toolkit.render.software_renderer's own header) -- kept that way for
# tidiness, not because it fixes anything (see the compiler-gap note just
# below: the real trigger was the METHOD NAME, not file layout).
#
# Same interface-plus-default-free-function shape as toolkit.render.renderer/
# software_renderer's own Renderer/default_renderer() (see that file's own
# comment for the compiler gap this works around: assigning a concrete class
# straight to an interface-typed field doesn't compile, only routing it
# through a function whose OWN return type is the interface does). A target
# that never calls register_url_opener() gets a safe no-op -- the same
# has_image_painter-style guard toolkit.ui.interp's ImagePainter already
# uses, so `<link>` degrades to "does nothing" rather than crashing on a
# freestanding/bare-metal build with no browser to open in the first place.
#
# A real compiler gap, found wiring this up: the interface method was
# originally named `open`, and having TWO OR MORE classes implement it
# (NoOpUrlOpener here, Canvas2DUrlOpener in toolkit.render.web.
# canvas2d_renderer, NativeUrlOpener in toolkit.platform.native_browser)
# made tauraroc's C backend emit a vtable struct initializer referencing
# `<Class>_open` for every implementer, but never actually emit the
# forward declaration for ANY of them -- "undeclared identifier" at
# compile time, not a link error, and the compiler's own fuzzy-match
# suggestion ("did you mean '_open'?") points at some unrelated CRT
# symbol, not a real fix. Reproduced in an 8-line minimal repro (two
# trivial classes implementing a 1-method interface named `open`, one
# registered into a holder, called through the interface field): fails
# exactly this way, on the very first two implementers, regardless of
# whether they share a file or not (tried splitting into separate files
# first -- same two symbols still undeclared, ruling out file layout as
# the trigger). Renaming the SAME interface method to `launch` (nothing
# else changed) compiles and dispatches correctly. Root cause not found
# (something about the specific identifier "open" is special-cased
# somewhere in codegen, plausibly for the `ClassName.open()` static-
# factory convention several backends already use, e.g. SdlCanvas.open()
# -- but that's a STATIC method, not an interface INSTANCE method, so this
# looks like a name check that doesn't distinguish the two). Worked around
# by naming this method `launch` instead; not fixed in the compiler
# itself -- worth reproducing standalone and reporting upstream.
pub interface UrlOpener:
    def launch(self, url: str) -> void

pub class NoOpUrlOpener implements UrlOpener:
    _unused: int

extend NoOpUrlOpener:
    pub def init() -> NoOpUrlOpener:
        mut o = NoOpUrlOpener()
        o._unused = 0
        return o

    pub def launch(self, url: str) -> void:
        pass

pub def default_url_opener() -> UrlOpener:
    return NoOpUrlOpener.init()
